# DEFERRED-GAP FIXTURE (#[ignore]'d — the N:1 scheduler does not treat `sleep`
# as a suspension point; see todo/t0822).
#
# `docs/book/14-concurrency.md` states the async model plainly: "tasks run
# cooperatively, and a task yields control at explicit suspension points:
# `await`, `sleep`, blocking I/O. At each such point, the runtime releases all
# held locks, RUNS OTHER TASKS, then reacquires the locks on resumption."
#
# Under `directive scheduler=single` (the N:1 cooperative event loop) `sleep`
# does NOT run other tasks: the spawned worker is still pending after a 100 ms
# sleep and only runs at `t.await()`. Under the default pool scheduler the same
# program runs the worker during the sleep, as documented.
#
# MEASURED at the filing commit, one pinned release binary, 40 runs at
# parallelism 4 per cell — every cell 40/40 identical, so this is a property,
# not a race:
#   --- pool   (both lanes): worker runs | after sleep | 1 | 1     ← documented
#   --- single (Rust lane) : after sleep | 0 | worker runs | 1     ← actual
#   --- single (self-host) : after sleep | 0 | worker runs | 1     ← same
# Both lanes agree because the scheduler runtime is shared C
# (`src/backend/c/runtime`), selected by `SchedulerMode` — this is a RUNTIME
# defect, not a lowering divergence, so there is no lane split to reconcile.
#
# Expected output below is the language-CORRECT, book-documented behaviour —
# i.e. what `scheduler=pool` already prints — NOT what `single` prints today.
# A program that sleeps while waiting for a spawned task to make progress
# cannot make that progress under `single`, which is a livelock shape, not a
# scheduling preference.

directive scheduler=single

from std.time import sleep

async void worker(int &counter):
    print("worker runs")
    counter = counter + 1

async void main():
    shared int x = 0
    Task[void] t = spawn worker(&x)
    sleep(0.1)
    print("after sleep")
    with x:
        print(x)
    t.await()
    print(x)
